{"text": "The Tower.\n\nOn Friday morning, the doctor took off the cast. Mateo's leg was thin and white. It felt strange and weak.\n\n\"I'm coming with you to the beach,\" said Mum. \"That's the rule.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Mateo. But Mum had a strange smile on her face. Did she know something?\n\nThey walked down the hill together.\n\nThe beach was full of people. In the middle of the beach, there was a tall white tower. At the top, there was a red chair.\n\nA woman stood at the bottom of the tower. She was tall, with grey hair under an old red hat. She had a whistle around her neck.\n\nShe looked at Mateo's leg. Then she looked at his face.\n\n\"Head up, Mateo,\" she said with a big smile.\n\nMateo stopped. He knew that voice. He knew that laugh.\n\n\"Ines?\" he said. \"You taught me to swim!\"\n\n\"Yes, I did,\" said Ines. \"You were a very brave little boy. You were afraid, but you never stopped.\"\n\n\"The photos were from your tower!\" said Mateo. \"It was you!\"\n\n\"And it was you!\" laughed Ines. \"I didn't know until today.\"\n\nMum laughed, too. \"I guessed last week. I heard 'Head up! Kick your legs!' all summer, nine years ago.\"\n\nThey sat on the sand next to the tower. Ines told them her story.\n\nFor thirty years, she watched the sea from this tower. Every morning, she took a photo for her little sister. But in May, her sister moved to a cold country far away.\n\n\"She missed the sea,\" said Ines. \"So I sent it to her. Then I sent it to the wrong number. But my best swimmer saw it.\"\n\n\"Is today really your last day?\" asked Mateo.\n\n\"Yes. A new lifeguard starts tomorrow.\" She looked at him. \"But next summer, we're going to train young volunteers. They help the lifeguards. Do you want to try?\"\n\nMateo looked at the sea. It was green and blue, and very quiet.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"Yes, please.\"\n\nThe next morning at seven, his phone made a small sound. There was a photo of the sea and a message.\n\n\"Good morning, little brother! Head up. 